## INTRODUCTION
Carbon dioxide shows vast potential as a gas-phase marker in natural, domestic, and industrial processes.1,2 For example, using the indoor $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level as a measure of human occupation can lead to smarter and more energy-efficient automation of buildings or vehicles.3 Future Internet of things (IoT) devices able to perceive precise $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ levels may find use in health and safety applications such as surveillance,4 food spoilage,5 and air quality.6
Nondispersive infrared (NDIR) $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ optical sensors are well-established in a handful of practical applications.7 However, even after decades of optimization, some inherent drawbacks of their optoelectronic mode of operation remain unresolved: device complexity, power consumption, scalability, and cost.1,2 To enable the widespread adoption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensors in many aspects of modern society, inexpensive massproducible devices are needed that offer simplicity, robustness, and ultralow power demand. Chemiresistors offer many of these benefits and are commercially already well-established in the form of metal oxide $\left(\operatorname{MOx}\right)$ sensors.8 The operation principle of these simple, two-terminal passive components is based on conducting materials that transduce analyte−surface interactions to shifts in their current−voltage $\left(I{-}V\right)$ characteristics. $I{-}V$ curves correlate chemical states (e.g., target gas partial pressures) to the direct-current (DC) conductance, $G,$ of a resistor (Figure 1a). However, in contrast to all combustible gases or vapors typically targeted by $\mathbf{MOx}$ sensors,9 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ detection by oxidative decomposition is not feasible because $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cannot be further oxidized.
Analogous to the recent advances made for other classes of challenging analytes,10 material developments on the molecular level are key to establishing practical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemiresistors. Typical ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing applications would require quantitative sensing in $10{-}1000~\mathrm{\ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increments relative to the $0.04\%$ (i.e., $400\ \mathrm{ppm},$ atmospheric level, in air under a wide range of relative humidity (RH). Although many avenues of molecular and nanoscopic materials have been explored,11,12 there are no materials that operate as efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemiresistors within these boundary conditions. One popular means to enhance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensitivity and selectivity of chemiresistors is through functionalization with amine-rich nonconducting polymers, such as polyethylenimine (PEI),13 which reversibly form acid−base adducts with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or catalyze the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to carbonic acid and bicarbonates.14−17 However, these composite amine-rich sensors display sharply decreasing sensitivity even for moderate drops in RH because dehydrated sensors gradually lose their ability to transduce interactions with CO2.15−19
Electrically conducting two-dimensional metal−organic frameworks (2D MOFs) are an emergent class of nanoporous hybrid materials built from redox-active organic ligands and transition metal inorganic nodes.20−22 The unique combination of electrical conductivity and intrinsic nanoporosity raises the intriguing potential to use 2D MOFs as chemical sensors.23,24 Their utility in this respect has already been demonstrated with redox-active gases or condensable vapors, but none of these prior studies have identified efficient materials for the particularly difficult $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ analyte, nor have they focused on addressing moisture as a significant interference.25−30
Herein, we propose a platform for ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing based on conducting 2D MOFs that are autogenously hydrated and comprise imino-semiquinonate (NH) moieties. As proof of concept, we demonstrate $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}$ (hexaiminobenzene) $_{2},$ ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{HIB}}_{2},$ sensors prepared by simple drop-casting of assynthesized polycrystalline material on interdigitated electrodes. Sensors made from $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ are capable of detecting small variations in the atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level. Their sensitivity in the 400−1000 ppm range, $-0.62\%$ $(100~\mathrm{\ppm})^{-1}$ , is remarkably invariant with relative humidity (RH) and is the highest among any other materials in the broad range $10{-}80\%$ RH. In situ diffuse-reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) points toward analyte-induced charge trapping as the mechanism behind the observed response of the sensors.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The recently reported $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ (Figure $\left|1\mathbf{b}\right\rangle^{31-33}$ combines favorable design elements for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemiresistive sensing: electrical conductivity through a conjugated backbone, Lewisbasic N-heteroatoms, and protons for hydrogen-bond stabilization of charge-separated species. Its small nanopores (idealized $0.8~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ diameter) could further provide a good balance between accessibility to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding sites and confined inclusion effects that combine to strongly enhance sensor− analyte interactions.14 Notably, among all known conducting 2D MOFs, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ is built from the smallest possible organic building unit and therefore exhibits the highest density of NH moieties.
Similar to amine-rich polymers, reversible formation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adducts or bicarbonate salts can be conceived within $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ when the material is exposed to ambient conditions. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ engaging in electron withdrawing interactions with amines, amounting to charge trapping with respect to the material, could give rise to direct chemiresistive modulation, as the amines are an integral part of the material’s conjugated backbone (Figure 1c). To explore the potential for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing, we first explored the water and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties of bulk $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ . In contrast with nonporous amine-rich polymers, guest-accessible channels are intrinsic to the nanoporous structure of the MOF, and they remain accessible even after repeated thermal activation and prolonged exposure to dry or ambient conditions (Figures S1). Revealingly, the experimental $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption isotherm for ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{HIB}}_{2}$ showed distinct monolayer uptake in the range from zero to $5\mathrm{-}10\%$ RH (Figures 1d and S2). Fitting the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sorption data to the Langmuir isotherm revealed monolayer coverage in the $0{-}20\%$ RH range corresponding to 8.1 wt $\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptake. This is consistent with filling of the subnanometer pores (approximately three $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules for each intrinsic cavity). By contrast, the much more gradual $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptake at higher RH is indicative of structure-extrinsic, multilayer adsorption on grain boundaries and other structural defects. Importantly, the data reveal that the intrinsic hydration level of the MOF, corresponding to water uptake within the pores, should remain unchanged at values of RH greater than approximately $10\%$ regardless of macroscopic sample morphology. The heat of adsorption for $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ above $10\%$ RH is relatively small, approximately $35{-}45\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , and indicates reversible physisorption as the key interaction mechanism. Because the heat of adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ itself is within the same range at low relative pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (approximately $35\mathrm{-}40\mathrm{kJ}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ; Figures 1e and S3), exchange of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ should occur without a significant energy penalty. Whereas the heat of adsorption values are small for water, as its spontaneous condensation on surfaces under these conditions of pressure and temperature is anticipated, the equivalent range is quite large for noncondensable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but consistent with the formation of acid−base adducts between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the Nheteroatoms in the pores of the 2D MOF.34 Moreover, singlecomponent isotherms often give lower-limit estimates of the ambient interactions, as markedly stronger interaction pathways can be anticipated for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and hydrated $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ (cf. bicarbonate formation, Figure 1c).
Chemiresistors were fabricated on interdigitated electrodes, by simply drop-casting as-synthesized suspensions of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ as inks. The devices were loaded into a custom-built gas sensing cell (see experimental description) and conditioned for several hours under constant electrical bias and simulated ambient conditions. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing performance of the devices was tested through direct-current amperometric $\left(I{-}t\right)$ measurements at room temperature. The devices were subjected to different levels of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the $400{-}2500\ \mathrm{ppm}$ range, i.e., from outdoors atmospheric level to the upper limit typically found indoors, in both increasing and decreasing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration regimes.35 Each measurement was conducted at various levels of humidity in the range $0\mathrm{-}80\%$ RH, and 10 individual devices were tested over a total period of approximately 1 week. The normalized $I{-}t$ traces, $G/G_{0},$ recorded for one of the 10 devices, are shown in Figure 2a ( $\stackrel{\cdot}{G}_{0}$ $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ initial baseline current). These experiments evidenced a clear “turn-off” response, i.e., inverse proportionality of the device conductance with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level, and demonstrated highly repeatable and robust operation over 7 days of continuous experimentation.
The normalized responses between 400 and $1000\mathrm{ppm}$ , $\Delta G/$ $G_{400\ p p\mathrm{m}},$ are plotted as a function of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dosage in Figure 2b (see also Figure S4; $G_{400}{\mathrm{\ppm}}=$ current after recovery from each respective incremental dose). Notably, over the broad range of ambient conditions $(10-80\%$ RH), the response of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ -based sensors is linear with respect to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level and essentially RH-independent. The interpolated sensitivity (S) is $-0.62\%$ $[100\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ of $\mathsf{C O}_{2})^{-1}$ , and the attainable signal resolution for a noise level of $3\sigma$ is 67 ppm. Despite the rather rudimentary nature of the drop-casted $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ sensors, their signal resolution is competitive with that of some commercial NDIR $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensors.7 Intriguingly, these results also highlight the critical role of moisture in the performance of the devices: at humidity levels below $10\%$ RH or under dry conditions, the sensitivity drops considerably. Such conditions are only found, however, during the driest days in the driest deserts, at very high altitudes, or in Antarctica.36
In Figure 2c, time-resolved analysis of the normalized response-recovery curves shows that the transient sensor characteristics reasonably obey first-order rate laws (i.e., approaching straight lines in the transformed semi-ln plot). The response and recovery times required to reach $90\%$ of the saturation values $\left({t_{90}}\right)$ were in the range of $7{-}11\ \mathrm{min}$ for a shift between 400 and $2000~\mathrm{ppm}$ and constant RH values between
10 and $80\%$ . Hence, the time resolution of the sensors is suitable for practical applications that require multiple measurements per hour. Consistent with the steady-state characteristics of the sensors, the normalized transient signals are nearly equivalent for the multiple simulated conditions of ambient RH. By contrast, the normalized kinetics were noticeably slower under simulated dry conditions $'_{t_{90}}=12-$ $13~\mathrm{min}$ at $0\%$ RH, dark green trace in Figure 2c). It should be noted, however, that even traces of water, which would be strongly adsorbed below $10\%$ RH, are likely to promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ interactions under these conditions.
Figure 2d highlights the versatility of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ as a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensor relative to other materials reported for this application. In the relatively narrow, but expanding field of MOF-based sensors (see Table S1), $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ is the only material capable of detecting practically relevant $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ levels regardless of the transduction mechanism. More relevantly, the sensitivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ is consistently the highest across the widest RH range reported thus far, even when compared to more complex sensor designs, e.g., meticulously downsized materials such as carbon nanotubes or few-layer graphene/graphene-oxide flakes and composite materials (see Table S2 for a detailed description of the entries in Figure 2d).
Even though long-term operation data beyond the range of a few days is seldom available for previous ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemiresistors (see Table S2), the long-term stability of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ sensorsbeyond the robust first week of operation shown in Figure 2awas probed by repeating the test sequence at $50\%$ RH on the same set of devices every 2 weeks for 3.5 months (Figure S5, dotted lines in Figure 2d). During this time, the sensitivity drifted, as is often observed with other chemiresistors.44 Despite this gradual drop in absolute sensitivity, the signals remained fully analytical and predictable, and the sensitivity after three months under simulated real-world conditions was still RH-independent (Figure S6).
The remarkable performance of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ as a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensor and several data in Figure 2, most obviously the divergent behavior under dry conditions, point to a unique $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture/ sensing mechanism that warrants further investigation. Indeed, the response of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ sensors at $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ levels below the atmospheric range (i.e., $0{-}500\ \mathrm{ppm})$ is both stronger, $S=$ $-3.4\%$ $(100\mathrm{ppm})^{-1}$ and slower, $t_{90}>2$ h (Figure S7). This is expected because the strongest adsorption sites are occupied by the first few molecules at a low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level. Although for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ these strong adsorption sites do not influence the sensitivity in the relevant range of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration (i.e., above $400\ \mathrm{\ppm})$ , these results show that tuning material properties to enhance sensitivity would likely come at the expense of response time.
To test the efficacy of related 2D conducting MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing, we investigated isomorphic materials made either from other transition metals or from differently functionalized organic ligands. Whereas all of these materials exhibited humidity responses similar to that of ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{HIB}}_{2},$ roughly $0.2-$ $0.5\%$ $(\%\ \mathrm{RH})^{-1}$ (Figure S8), none exhibited $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensitivity approaching that of the latter (Figure 2d, and Figures $S9\mathrm{-}11\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ). Thus, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{HOTP}\ =\ 2,3,6,7,10,11\right.$ -hexaoxytriphenylene), wherein copper nodes bridge semiquinonate (O) rather than imino-semiquinonate (NH) ligands, showed no measurable response at any $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level. This confirms the important role of the imines in promoting interactions with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ . ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{3}{\mathrm{HIB}}_{2},$ the isostructural Ni analogue of ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{HIB}}_{2},$ displayed “turn-on” responses, i.e., conductivity changes that were opposite in sign to those observed for the Cu analogue, that were nevertheless relatively small and were further negatively affected by increasing RH. $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ $\left({\mathrm{HITP}}\ =\ \right.$ 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminosemiquinonatotriphenylene), an iminecontaining MOF with a wider pore size, showed similar weak sensing characteristics. Notably, all imine materials exhibit a similar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure (Figure S12), suggesting that the vastly superior sensitivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ relative to $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ is intrinsic and is likely related to electronic effects, as also addressed below.
Clues to the mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ came from investigating powder samples exposed to various levels of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $50\%$ RH by in situ diffuse-reflectance infrared Fouriertransform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) (Figure 3a). These spectra revealed important information about material stability as well as the first indications of electronic modulation of the MOF in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Thus, the vibrational modes pertaining to the organic ligands and the MOF lattice remained invariable at any level of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ exposure, underlining the chemical stability of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ under humid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere. Similarly, the frequencies of the characteristic $\nu_{2}$ and $\nu_{3}$ modes of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ were nearly indistinguishable from those of gaseous $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ with only their intensities varying consistent with adsorptive accumulation (Figure S13). No characteristic modes for bicarbonate or carbamates were observed at $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentrations between $0{-}2500~\mathrm{ppm}$ ; these modes became apparent only weakly and only upon significantly raising the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level to $1-10\%$ (i.e., $10000{-}100000\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ; Figure S14). Analogous to the speciation of dissolved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in aqueous amine media,45 the bicarbonate and carbamate salt fractions in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ are presumed to be exceedingly small, particularly at typical ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ levels. Most revealingly, increasing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ levels cause a gradual decrease of the background IR absorbance of the material, most evident in the spectral ranges that are not dominated by strong vibrational modes from the MOF itself (e.g., ${\mathrm{h}}\nu=0.5{-0.6}{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and $0.25{-}0.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ . In degenerate or narrow-gap semiconductors, the background IR absorbance is generally associated with intraband excitation of free carriers in partially filled bands, also known as free carrier absorption (FCA).46 FCA evolves inversely proportional with the square of the photon energy. Here, the decrease of background IR intensity with increasing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level is in line with a decrease of the free carrier concentration in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ .
The modulation of the free carrier concentration in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is corroborated by diffuse reflectance data in the nearinfrared region. As shown in Figure 3b, the diminished background absorbance induced by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ persists above the IR range up to approximately $1.2\ \mathrm{{\eV}_{\mathrm{{i}}}}$ , in agreement with the DRIFTS data. Qualitatively, the same data can be interpreted as a blue shift of the optical absorption edge, i.e., a widening of the optical band gap (Figure 3b, inset).
The above treatment is in good agreement with the experimental NIR data transformed in Tauc coordinates for indirect interband transitions, $(\alpha h\nu)^{0.5}$ , assuming proportionality between $F(R)$ and $\alpha$ , the absorption coefficient. This assumption can be made through dilution of the sample in an appropriate matrix. The linearized range of the Tauc plots near the midpoint between the low and high absorbance rangeswas fitted and extrapolated to the intersection with the $h\nu$ -axis (Figure 3d).47,49,50 Through the shifting intersect, a clear widening of the optical gap, $\Delta E_{0}/E_{0}=+2-5\%$ or 13−32 meV, is observed for exposure to $500{-}2500\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Notably, the incremental widening of the gap shows distinct saturation toward higher levels, consistent with the shape of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm and with the chemiresistive characteristics of the material (Figure 3d). Notably, similar absorbance background/band edge shifts are observable at different $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ratios, consistent with the selective response to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under hydrated conditions (Figures S15). Importantly, although these data do not provide a molecularlevel understanding of a particular $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding mode to ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{HIB}}_{2},$ they are consistent with the hypothesis of charge trapping as a mechanism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing, as proposed earlier. Indeed, water also induces background changes to the optical absorption data, but only below $10\%\mathrm{RH}_{\cdot}$ , which we correlate with intrinsic water sorption. Above $10\%$ RH, no significant further background modulation is observed (Figure S16), which is consistent with the water adsorption isotherm indicating extrinsic adsorption at $\mathrm{RH}>10\%$ . Notably, the low absorbance spectral region between the FCA and the onset of the absorption edge is not observed for $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HIB}_{2}$ (Figure S17). The absence of this optical signature confirms the different electronic origin of the much weaker “turn-on” response of this 2D MOF.
In conclusion, conducting MOFs based on imino-semiquinonate moieties provide a powerful platform for chemiresistive sensing of ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Autogenous hydration of nanopores under ambient conditions provides remarkable independence of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensitivity with relative humidity, which contrasts strongly with previously reported $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemiresistors. Supported by electrical and spectroscopic data, we showed that compositional tuning of the 2D MOFs greatly impacts the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensitivity. Encouraged by the robustness of our proof-of-concept sensors throughout months of use, and by the modularity and flexibility of the conducting 2D MOFs platform, these results provide a blueprint for tuning and optimizing chemiresistive responses for other host−guest pairs of interest.
## METHODS
Device Preparation. Interdigitated electrodes $\left(\mathrm{Pt},200\mu\mathrm{m}\right.$ line width; $150\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ interspacing) on ceramic supports were obtained from BVT Technologies (product ID: CC1.W2). All 2D MOFs samples were prepared according to slightly modified published procedures, as described in the Supporting Information. No unexpected or unusually high safety hazards were encountered. Colloidal inks ( $\bar{3}\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ in methanol) were prepared by ultrasonication using the freshly prepared, nondried 2D MOF powders. The inks were manually dropcasted on the active area of the substrate, by $1{-}2\mu\mathrm{L}$ amounts, at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a hot plate and until the device resistances roughly stopped decreasing (typically, $_{5-10}$ droplets). The devices were baked for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stored under ambient conditions in closed gel-packs.
Gas Sensor Characterization. The gas sensor characterization was conducted at room temperature for up to 10 parallel devices using a home-built system consisting of a polytetrafluoroethylene cell and three Alicat mass-flow controllers. The Alicat Flow Vision software was used for programmed mixing of Airgas Ultra Zero synthetic air, synthetic air humidified using a scrubber filled with deionized water, and Airgas Research grade Carbon dioxide. A constant flow of 1 standard liter per minute (SLPM) was provided at the inlet of the cell (volume approximately $25~\mathrm{mL}$ ), and a mineral oil trap was connected to the outlet. The DC bias of the sensors was $0.8\mathrm{~V~},$ , and the currents were recorded in PalmSens PSTrace using a Palmsens EMstat3 equipped with a MUX-16 multiplexer (data acquisition rate: $0.5\ \mathrm{Hz}\$ ). Data analysis was conducted using a script in OriginLab OriginPro. Prior to the measurements, the devices were exposed to repeated hourly cycles of $0{-}500{-}1000~\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $50~\mathrm{RH}$ until the baseline current stabilized (approximately residual drift smaller than $-1\%\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ during the first 2 days, and smaller than $-0.2\%\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ during the following weeks).
Adsorption Isotherms. The water and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were collected for 2D MOF powder samples (approximately $35~\mathrm{{\mg}}$ dry weight, preactivated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under dynamic vacuum) using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 equipped with vapor dosing option and an isothermal circulation bath for temperature control. The isosteric enthalpies of adsorption were calculated through evaluation of the temperature dependence of the pressure at equal surface coverage, using the Clausius−Clapeyron equation and measurement of the isotherms at three temperatures (15, 20, $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot$ ).
In Situ Spectroscopy. Diffuse reflectance experiments were performed on carefully grinded mixtures of approximately 0.5 wt $\%$ 2D MOF in potassium bromide. All spectra are plotted with respect to the KBr background and after Kubelka−Munk transformation.
